<DocsLayout
  title="Security"
  description="Fail-closed defaults for SSH, secrets, approvals, rollback, and audit integrity."
  path="/docs/security"
>
  <p>
    OpsRun is built for incident response: it will run powerful commands on remote
    hosts. Defaults aim to make silent skips and invisible side effects hard.
  </p>

  <h2 id="validate">Validate before execute</h2>
  <p>
    Nothing runs until the runbook is parsed and validated. Operators can always use
    <code>--dry-run</code> to preview. Denylist matches fail closed.
  </p>

  <h2 id="ssh">SSH host keys</h2>
  <p>
    Verification against <code>known_hosts</code> is mandatory. Bypassing it requires an
    explicit flag and prints a loud warning. Prefer updating known_hosts.
  </p>

  <h2 id="secrets">Secrets</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Declare sensitive vars with <code>secret: true</code>.</li>
    <li>Resolve values through the secret provider (env prefix / files).</li>
    <li>
      Real values are used only in the command sent to the host. Approvals, TUI, events,
      and audit entries show <code>***REDACTED***</code>.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="approvals">Approvals</h2>
  <p>
    Modes <code>per_step</code> and <code>all</code> force a human to see the
    interpolated command and target list before execution. Use
    <code>confirm: true</code> on destructive steps even when the runbook default is
    looser.
  </p>

  <h2 id="rollback">Rollback</h2>
  <p>
    Destructive patterns without an <code>opsrun:rollback</code> block produce
    validator warnings. On failure, completed destructive steps reverse in dependency
    order.
  </p>

  <h2 id="audit">Audit integrity</h2>
  <p>
    The audit log is append-only JSONL. Each record embeds the SHA-256 of the previous
    record. Optional Ed25519 signing strengthens non-repudiation. Verify with:
  </p>
  <pre><code>opsrun audit verify --log ./audit/opsrun-audit.jsonl</code></pre>

  <h2 id="privilege">Least privilege</h2>
  <p>
    No persistent agent or always-elevated daemon on targets. Standard SSH is enough.
    Use non-interactive sudo only for the commands that need it.
  </p>

  <h2 id="reporting">Vulnerability reporting</h2>
  <p>
    Do not file public issues for security bugs. Follow
    <a href="https://github.com/Leo-Galli/opsrun/blob/main/SECURITY.md" rel="noopener noreferrer" target="_blank">SECURITY.md</a>.
  </p>
</DocsLayout>
